NNAAMMEE
     jjeexxeecc — execute a command inside an existing jail

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     jjeexxeecc [--ll] [--dd _w_o_r_k_i_n_g_-_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y] [--uu _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e | --UU _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e] _j_a_i_l
           [_c_o_m_m_a_n_d _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The jjeexxeecc utility executes _c_o_m_m_a_n_d inside the _j_a_i_l identified by its jid
     or name.  If _c_o_m_m_a_n_d is not specified then the user's shell is used.

     The following options are available:

     --dd _w_o_r_k_i_n_g_-_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
             The working directory for running commands inside the jail.  The
             default is the jail root directory.

     --ll      Execute in a clean environment.  The environment is discarded ex‐
             cept for HOME, SHELL, TERM, USER, and anything from the login
             class capability database for the user.  PATH is set to
             "/bin:/usr/bin".  If a user is specified (via --uu or --UU), and ab‐
             sent the --dd option, commands are run from that (possibly jailed)
             user's directory.

     --uu _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e
             The user name from host environment as whom the _c_o_m_m_a_n_d should
             run.  This is the default.

     --UU _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e
             The user name from jailed environment as whom the _c_o_m_m_a_n_d should
             run.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
   EExxaammppllee 11: OOppeenn aa sshheellll iinn aa jjaaiill
     The following command specifies a jail by its name and utilizes the cur‐
     rent user's shell:

           # jexec name

     It is also possible to specify a jail by its jid:

           # jexec JID

   EExxaammppllee 22: RRuunn aa ssiinnggllee ccoommmmaanndd wwiitthhoouutt ooppeenniinngg aa sshheellll
     The following command runs ‘uname -a’ in a jail called “name”.  Since a
     command is specified explicitly, jjeexxeecc does not spawn an interactive
     shell.  Instead, jjeexxeecc executes the specified command directly.

           # jexec name uname -a

   EExxaammppllee 33: OOppeenn aa sshheellll iinn aa jjaaiill wwiitthh aa cclleeaann eennvviirroonnmmeenntt
     The following command opens a sh(1) shell in a jail with a clean environ‐
     ment:

           # jexec -l name sh

   EExxaammppllee 44: OOppeenn aa sshheellll iinn aa jjaaiill wwiitthh tthhee llooggiinn ccoommmmaanndd
     The following command utilizes login(1) to access the jail, submitting an
     audit record, and displaying the user's last login, system copyright, and
     motd(5) message:

           # jexec -l name login -f root

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     jail_attach(2), jail(8), jls(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The jjeexxeecc utility was added in FreeBSD 5.1.

BBUUGGSS
     If the jail is not identified by _j_i_d there is a possible race in between
     the lookup of the jail and executing the command inside the jail.  Giving
     a _j_i_d has a similar race as another process can stop the jail and start
     another one after the user looked up the _j_i_d.

